Not quite.  From resist(mtmp, oclass, damage, tell) in zap.c,

alev = u.ulevel when casting a spell.  At best case, player is level
30, so I'll go with that.  dlev = monster level when monster level is
between 1 and 50.  The arch lich is level 37, so dlev = 37.

Then, when rn2(100 + alev - dlev) < monsters intrinsic MR, which for
an arch-lich is 90, the monster resists the affect.  Here, the check
is rn2(100 + 30 - 37) < 90, or rn2(93) < 90.  rn2(x) has a range of
0..x-1, so there's a 3 in 93 (~3.2%) chance the arch-lich will not
resist (when rn2(93) returns 90, 91, or 92).

Note that, for a character of level 27 or less, there is no chance to
tame this level 37 arch-lich, as you'll be comparing rn2(90) < 90
which is always true, so the arch-lich always resists.

Also note that a scroll of taming will work with alev = 9, not
u.ulevel.  This means that an arch-lich, which starts off at level 24,
can never be tamed with a normal scroll of taming (not going into
confused/blessed effects, not sure that they matter) as rn2(100 + 9 -
24) = rn2(85) which is always less than the arch-lich's MR of 90.

